''traverse, a.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Lying or being across or in a cross direction; cross; thwart.

{{smaller block|A kettle, slung<br>Between two poles upon a stick ''transverse.''
{{right|''Cowper'', Task, i. 561.}}}}

'''2.''' Collateral. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|When once it goes to the ''transverse'' and collateral [line], they not only have no title to the inheritance, but every remove is a step to the losing the cognation and relation to the chief house. ''Jer. Taylor'', Rule of Conscience, ii. 3.}}

'''3.''' In ''anat.'' and ''zoöl.'', broader or wider than long; having its major diameter crosswise: noting various parts or organs which lie or are taken to run across other parts, or especially across the long axis of the whole body. See ''transversalis'' and ''transversus.''—'''4.''' In ''bot.'': (''a'') Right and left or collateral with reference to the median plane. (''b'') Being at right angles to the axial direction: for example, see ''transverse partition'', below.—'''5.''' In ''herpet.'', specifically noting a bone of the skull

readings.c3s2:
which usually unites the palatine and the pterygoid bones with the maxilla. {{smaller|It is usually flattened, plate-like, and firmly sutured, making a solid framework of the maxillary and pterygopalatine bars; but in some ophidians, as the venomous snakes, it is a slender rod movably articulated in front with the maxilla, and connected behind with the pterygoid only; it then takes great part in the peculiar movement of the bones of the upper jaw by which the venom-fangs are thrown into position for striking. See also cuts under ''Ophidia'', ''Pythonidæ'', ''Crotalus'', and ''acrodont''.}}

6. In ''her.'', crossing the escutcheon from one side to the opposite one.—'''By transverse†''', confusedly; out of the proper order.

